[quote="Foote"]

It has very little haha

[/quote]

&nbsp;

So really more of a demo/teaser for the analog game, then?

&nbsp;

One of the things I like best about BCon is the strategic depth. &nbsp;Similar to Chess/Go/Checkers, there is *no* element of luck. &nbsp;Everything that happens in the game is a result of a player decision. &nbsp;There is minimal hidden knowledge (the only thing hidden is what attack each opponent is playing right before revealing). &nbsp;You always know exactly what cards your opponent has in hand and exactly what their capabilities are.

&nbsp;

There is a really deep mind-game involved as well. &nbsp;

Player 1: &quot;I have this awesome attack&quot;

Player 2: &quot;I know you have that awesome attack, but I have this perfect counter&quot;

Player 1: &quot;Yes, but I have this different attack that could counter your counter&quot;

Something of an &quot;I know that you know that I know that you know...&quot;, but it really allows for a lot of strategic and tactical play, and even defeats are very satisfying.

&nbsp;

Often, when I lose a competitive game, it is because of a bad dice roll, or a poor card flip (can even happen in Sentinels). &nbsp;However, a loss in BattleCON is either because I made a mistake or because my opponent outplayed me. &nbsp;Each defeat makes me a better player.

&nbsp;

BattleCON cannot replace Sentinels, but in our house it has fired every other competitive game. &nbsp;(BattleCON and Sentinels are the only two games we regularly keep in an easily accessible space in the living room. &nbsp;All other games are in the closet in the back with the Hard Hat sign on the door).
